1. The encoder-side licence

The encoder-side sources are dual licensed:

- You may use them under the GNU General Public License, version 3 or (at
  your option) any later version.
- Alternatively, you may use them under a separate commercial licence
  agreement with mtq0605, if you have concluded one.

Without such an agreement, only the GNU GPL applies. See COMMERCIAL.md.

2. Combined works

Encoder-side programs may be built together with decoder-side files. Those
files remain under Apache-2.0 and keep their headers.

A program that contains encoder-side code and is distributed under the GNU
GPL is, as a whole, under GPL-3.0-or-later. The Apache License 2.0 is
compatible with version 3 of the GNU GPL.

Whoever distributes such a program must also comply with section 4 of the
Apache License 2.0 for the decoder-side files. This includes passing on the
NOTICE file.

Decoder-side files must not include, or depend on, encoder-side files.

3. Output of the encoder

Audio files, bitstreams, logs and any other output produced by running the
LAE encoder, modified or unmodified, belong to whoever produced them.
mtq0605 claims no rights in such output.

Output may contain material from the encoder that is protected by
copyright, for example tables or header templates copied into a file. To
that extent, mtq0605 grants every person a perpetual, worldwide,
royalty-free and irrevocable permission to use, copy, modify and distribute
that output under any terms, without any obligation under the GNU GPL.

- The permission covers material in which mtq0605 holds the copyright.
- Material contributed by others is available under the Apache License
  2.0, under which it was contributed.
- The permission is separate from, and in addition to, the GNU GPL.

4. Implementing the format

The author does not require any licence, permission or fee for
implementing the LAE format. CC-BY-4.0 governs only the copying and
adapting of the specification and documentation texts. Anyone may write an
independent encoder or decoder for LAE under any licence.

5. Patents

CC-BY-4.0 and CC0-1.0 grant no patent rights. Section 3 of the Apache
License 2.0 and section 11 of the GNU GPL v3 contain patent licences from
contributors. The author's patent pledge is in PATENTS.md.

6. Names

None of these licences grants any right in the following names:

- "LAE"
- "Layered Audio Extension"
- "LAE Lossy"
- "LAE Lossless"
- "LAEY"
- "LAES"

You may use these names to state truthfully that a product implements LAE
or is derived from it.

7. No warranty

Each work is provided without warranty, as stated in its licence.

8. Contact

Contact is through GitHub.

- Open an issue in the LAE repository.
- For private matters, use the repository's private vulnerability
  reporting (Security tab).
